The algorithms are written from their published descriptions.

The DE-9IM matrix and the named predicates follow:

  OGC 06-103r4 (2011). OpenGIS Implementation Standard for Geographic
  Information - Simple Feature Access - Part 1: Common Architecture,
  version 1.2.1. Open Geospatial Consortium.

Area and length are accumulated by the compensated dot product of:

  Ogita, T., Rump, S.M., Oishi, S. (2005). Accurate Sum and Dot Product.
  SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing, 26(6), 1955-1988.

Simplification follows:

  Ramer, U. (1972). An Iterative Procedure for the Polygonal Approximation
  of Plane Curves. Computer Graphics and Image Processing, 1(3), 244-256.

  Douglas, D.H., Peucker, T.K. (1973). Algorithms for the Reduction of the
  Number of Points Required to Represent a Digitized Line or its
  Caricature. The Canadian Cartographer, 10(2), 112-122.

  Visvalingam, M., Whyatt, J.D. (1993). Line Generalisation by Repeated
  Elimination of Points. The Cartographic Journal, 30(1), 46-51.

The convex hull is Andrew's monotone chain:

  Andrew, A.M. (1979). Another Efficient Algorithm for Convex Hulls in Two
  Dimensions. Information Processing Letters, 9(5), 216-219.

The Delaunay triangulation is built by incremental insertion, with a
visibility walk to locate each point:

  Bowyer, A. (1981). Computing Dirichlet Tessellations. The Computer
  Journal, 24(2), 162-166.

  Watson, D.F. (1981). Computing the n-dimensional Delaunay Tessellation
  with Application to Voronoi Polytopes. The Computer Journal, 24(2),
  167-172.

  Devillers, O., Pion, S., Teillaud, M. (2002). Walking in a
  Triangulation. International Journal of Foundations of Computer Science,
  13(2), 181-199.

The discrete Frechet distance follows:

  Eiter, T., Mannila, H. (1994). Computing Discrete Frechet Distance.
  Technical Report CD-TR 94/64, Christian Doppler Laboratory for Expert
  Systems, TU Wien.

The count of a polygon's interior components rests on Alexander duality:

  Hatcher, A. (2002). Algebraic Topology. Cambridge University Press,
  Theorem 3.44.

The concave hull and coverage simplification follow the methods of the JTS
Topology Suite (Martin Davis), ConcaveHull and CoverageSimplifier, as
described in its documentation. JTS is licensed under the Eclipse Public
License 2.0 and the Eclipse Distribution License 1.0.
